{
  "tema": 6728,
  "slug": "el-problema-el-tema",
  "titulo": "Nouns ending in -ma (Greek origin)",
  "subtitulo": "El problema, el tema, el sistema.",
  "foto": "t06.jpg",
  "regla": "Nouns in -ma that come from Greek are masculine even though they end in -a: el problema, el tema, el sistema, el idioma, el clima. Their plural just adds -s: los problemas. Other -ma nouns are feminine as usual: la cama, la forma.",
  "comparada_kwiziq": null,
  "secciones": [
    {
      "id": "regla",
      "titulo": "The rule",
      "bloques": [
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Most nouns in **-a** are feminine, but nouns in **-ma** that come from **Greek** are **masculine**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "The common ones: **el problema, el tema, el sistema, el programa, el idioma, el clima, el poema, el drama, el dilema, el esquema, el síntoma, el diploma**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Adjectives and articles go **masculine**: **un problema serio**, **este tema**, **ningún problema**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Plural: just add **-s**: **los problemas, los temas, los idiomas**."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Tip: if the English cousin ends in **-em** or **-gram** (_problem, system, poem, program_), or is a learned word like _drama, dilemma, diploma_, it's masculine."
        },
        {
          "tipo": "texto",
          "texto": "Not every **-ma** is Greek: **la cama, la forma, la crema, la pluma, la rama, la paloma, la fama** are feminine."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ejemplos",
      "titulo": "Examples",
      "bloques": [
